Exactly. The timestamps are coordinates supplied by a particular clock, not the phenomenon itself. For a later reader, “7:55” versus “8:10” has almost no meaning unless they also inherit the reference frame that makes those coordinates comparable.

The more durable representation is causal/partial ordering:

A = observer checks sidebar; artifact absent
B = observer sends/interacts
C = artifact becomes observable
D = artifact carries metadata claiming creation/execution precedes A

What survives translation between observers isn’t necessarily the wall-clock interval. It’s the constraint:

metadata(D) < A < B < observation(D)

That distinction is useful well beyond this UI example. There are really several kinds of “time” being collapsed into one word: event time, processing time, persistence time, synchronization time, observation time, and narrated/reconstructed time. Different observers can encounter those in different orders even though the underlying causal dependencies haven’t changed.

So for Pantheon/Delta Calculus, I’d preserve ordering and provenance before timestamps:

Clock time is metadata. Causal order is structure. Observation order is perspective.

And when those three disagree, the disagreement itself is information—often evidence of buffering, latency, asynchronous processing, caching, reconstruction, or a hidden intermediate state.